<div class="md" data-lesson-id="information_market">
We speak of "the information age" as if information became valuable only recently. It did not. Information has been a **commodity** — something produced, shipped, priced, and traded — for centuries. But a commodity is not just "valuable things"; a commodity is valuable *because it moves*. It has producers, a price, a channel, and buyers. So the question this chapter asks is not "when did information become important?" but **how was a *market* for it built** — because a market, with a flow, is the thing everything after it (the encyclopedia, the database, the web) runs on.
</div>

<div class="md">
## Compiling knowledge before the web

### News becomes a commodity: the Fugger network (16th c.)

The first layer is the **market** itself. In the sixteenth century, in the banking city of Augsburg, the Fugger family ran one of Europe's largest information networks \cite[the Fugger news market, Wikipedia]{fuggernews}. Bankers and merchants lived on *information*: a rumor of war, a price in Venice, a ship lost at sea — any of these was money, and it was money that *decayed*. So the Fuggers ran a web of correspondents across Europe. A letter describing a significant event was copied and circulated as a news report; and a common device was a plain listing of current prices, the circulation of which quickened international trade \cite[News — early news networks, Wikipedia]{fuggernews}.

This is the *Relation*, the German newssheet — and it is the first place we can point to news as a **product**: something a producer makes, ships out, and a buyer receives. Information has become the thing that is *sold*.

<figure>
	<img style="max-width: 320px; width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; margin: 0 auto;" src="relation_1609.jpg" alt="Title page of a German Relation (newssheet), 1609" />
	<figcaption class="md">Title page of a German *Relation* — the earliest surviving sheet of the genre, here Johann Carolus's Strasbourg *Relation* (1605/1609), recognized as the world's first newspaper. The Fugger network of Augsburg dealt in this same form of news. Public domain (University of Heidelberg scan).</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>

<div class="md">
### The coffee-house public sphere

A commodity needs a **public**. In the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries the venue was the coffee house. The sociologist \citeauthor{habermaspublicsphere} (\citeyear{habermaspublicsphere}) later named what was happening there: the **public sphere** — a space that was physically a coffee house in Britain, a salon in France, a reading society in Germany, where private individuals gathered to talk about *printed matter* as a shared reality \cite[the coffee-house public sphere, Habermas]{habermaspublicsphere}.

Notice what is new. The authority is not in any single person's rank, title, or lineage. It is in the **forum**: you argued a point, but you argued it to a public that had *read the same paper*. Information stopped being a private possession in the head of the Fugger banker and became the raw material of a collective, argument-generating public. The news sheet (the commodity) and the coffee house (the venue) are two halves of the same invention: a way for a *society* to share and contest what it knows.

\marginfig{coffee_house_1674.png}{A London coffee house, 1674 — the physical venue of the early public sphere. Public domain engraving.}
</div>

<div class="md">
### Before the Encyclopédie: Isidore of Seville

The urge to gather *all* knowledge into one place is far older than the Enlightenment. Nearly eleven centuries before Diderot, in Visigothic Spain, the archbishop \cite[Isidore of Seville]{isidoreofseville} (c. 560–636) compiled the *\citetitle{etymologiae}* — a twenty-book encyclopedia that tried to capture the whole of ancient learning in a single ordered work. Its modern translators call it "arguably the most influential book, after the Bible, in the learned world of the Latin West for nearly a thousand years" \cite{etymologiae}.

Isidore did not merely store the classics; he reorganized them. Drawing on hundreds of earlier authors — Pliny, Solinus, Servius, the church fathers — he sorted everything from grammar, rhetoric, and the mathematical *quadrivium* to medicine, law, animals, geography, and the making of furniture into twenty books, each a folder in one universal filing cabinet \cite{etymologiae}. The *Etymologiae* became *the* textbook of the Middle Ages, copied in nearly a thousand surviving manuscripts; so completely did it stand in for the originals that some classical works stopped being copied and were lost, surviving only as his excerpts \cite{etymologiae}.

It is a fitting accident of history, then, that the Catholic Church has come to regard Isidore as the patron saint of the internet — and of computer users and programmers. The attribution traces to Pope John Paul II, who around 1997 proposed the compiler of the first great encyclopedia as a guardian for the network of all knowledge; the sources disagree over whether the gesture was ever made a formal decree, which is itself a very Isidorean sort of uncertainty (\cite[Isidore of Seville, Wikipedia]{isidoreofseville}). A seventh-century bishop who tried to put the whole world into twenty books is, at the very least, the right ancestor for the thing this chapter is about.
</div>

<div class="md">
## The Encyclopédie and the Mundaneum

### The Encyclopédie: compiling everything at once

If the coffee house was where information was *discussed*, the Encyclopédie was the great eighteenth-century attempt to **collect** it. Between 1751 and 1772, \citeauthor{encyclopedie} and some hundred and forty contributors set out to put *all* known knowledge into a single work — the sciences, the arts, the trades — and, crucially, to **cross-reference** the articles to one another: a device the history of the book calls "a kind of precursor to the hyperlink" \cite[the Encyclopédie, Diderot & d'Alembert]{encyclopedie}.

The ambition was not merely to *store* knowledge but to *connect* it — and in doing so, to change how people thought. This is the **corpus** in its first systematic form: a body of text, organized and internally linked, large enough that it becomes an object in its own right. You can now point at "the Encyclopédie" the way you later point at "the web" — as a single, structured, interlinked thing. Two centuries after Diderot, that object has a new name: the *training corpus* \cite[the Encyclopédie, Diderot & d'Alembert]{encyclopedie}.

